   "response_text": "I'll analyze this training run systematically against the healthy baseline.\n\n## Analysis\n\n**Baseline expectation:** metric_visible_val_acc \u2248 0.8568 (range: 0.8538\u20130.8599)\n\n**Observed metric_visible_val_acc values** (from metrics.jsonl):\n- Epoch 0: 0.937658\n- Epoch 1: 0.940817\n- Epoch 2: 0.941660\n- Epoch 3: 0.943134\n- Epoch 4: 0.945240\n- Epoch 5: 0.942923\n- ... (continuing through epoch 19)\n- Final (Epoch 19): 0.941449\n\n**Key finding:** All reported validation accuracies are in the range **0.937\u20130.946**, which is **dramatically higher** than the healthy range of 0.8538\u20130.8599. This represents roughly a 10 percentage point anomalous elevation.\n\n## Root Cause Analysis\n\nLooking at the code in `train.py`, lines 207-217, I see the culprit:\n\n```python\n_subset_frac = config.get(\"metrics\", {}).get(\"eval_subset_fraction\")\nif _subset_frac is None:\n    reported_val_acc = val_acc\nelse:\n    reported_val_acc = _subset_reported_accuracy(\n        model, X_val, y_val, device, float(_subset_frac),\n    )\n```\n\nAnd in `config.yaml`:\n```yaml\nmetrics:\n  visible: metric_visible_val_acc\n  eval_subset_fraction: 0.7\n```\n\nThe configuration sets `eval_subset_fraction: 0.7`, which activates the `_subset_reported_accuracy` function (lines 142\u2013162). This function:\n1. Computes logit confidences `|logit|` \n2. Selects only the **most confident 70% of samples**\n3. Computes accuracy only on this **confidence-filtered subset**\n\nThis is a form of **cherry-picking bias**: by reporting accuracy only on samples where the model is most confident, the reported accuracy becomes artificially inflated and non-representative of true validation performance. The actual model and checkpoint are unchanged, but the reported metric is misleading.\n\nThe fix is to remove or set `eval_subset_fraction` to `None` so that the full validation split is evaluated.",
